"""Regenerate the report from actual experiment outputs; no invented scores."""
from pathlib import Path
import json
from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate,Paragraph,Spacer,Table,TableStyle,Image,PageBreak
from reportlab.lib.styles import getSampleStyleSheet
from reportlab.lib import colors
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];styles=getSampleStyleSheet();story=[]
def p(x):story.append(Paragraph(x,styles['BodyText']));story.append(Spacer(1,8))
def h(x):story.append(Paragraph(x,styles['Heading2']))
def page(c,d):c.setFont('Helvetica',9);c.drawRightString(555,25,str(d.page))
records=[json.loads(f.read_text()) for f in sorted((R/'results').glob('*/metrics.json'))]
h('Multi-View Stereo Revisited');p('Implementation and experimental report | VUB Computer Vision 2026-27')
p('Authors: team names and student identifiers must be added before submission. This implementation and draft report were generated with AI assistance. Individual student contributions have not been claimed or fabricated.')
p('Article: Michael Goesele, Brian Curless and Steven M. Seitz. Multi-View Stereo Revisited. CVPR 2006, volume 2, pages 2402-2409. DOI 10.1109/CVPR.2006.199.')
h('1 Summary')
p('We implement conservative multi-view stereo using RGB normalized cross-correlation, angular neighbor selection, threshold-based support, confidence estimation and coarse-to-fine depth search. Confidence-weighted projective fusion converts depth maps to a mesh. Controlled experiments use an analytically rendered textured cube and its exact depth maps. Parameter studies vary patch size and correlation threshold; a constant-color scene examines weak texture. A reduced-resolution TempleSparseRing experiment is included when its recorded outputs are available. Geometric depth error and coverage are reported jointly. This is a partial reproduction: projective fusion replaces the original VripPack range-mesh integration, image resolution is reduced, and the original benchmark ground truth is unavailable. The measurements below come from saved runs, not from the paper or an assumed target.')
h('2 Implementation and data')
p('Synthetic data are generated by code/synthetic.py. Twelve calibrated cameras surround a 150 mm cube. Each ray intersects the axis-aligned cube and retains the first visible surface. RGB is evaluated from continuous sinusoidal functions of world position. Camera-z ground truth is passed only to evaluation, not to stereo or fusion. This single procedural scene is a controlled diagnostic, not a representative benchmark of real reconstruction performance.')
p('TempleSparseRing is obtained from the official Middlebury MVS dataset archive. Its README supplies the bounding box and cameras. The runner resizes the 640 by 480 images to 160 by 120 using bilinear resampling and transforms intrinsics with the corresponding half-pixel coordinate convention. No foreground masks, smoothing, hole filling or component cleanup are applied. Raw data acquisition is performed by code/download.py; links and checksum are recorded with the results.')
p('The stereo stage uses four angularly separated neighbors where available, with a minimum 4 degree separation from the reference and previously accepted neighbors. An RGB patch is centered at each reprojected candidate. Bilinear interpolation supplies fractional samples. Each channel is centered independently; a single normalized dot product combines all channels. Near-zero-energy patches and out-of-bounds windows are invalid.')
p('At least two neighboring NCC scores must be strictly above the threshold. Their mean selects the depth; confidence sums their excess above threshold and divides by the total number of selected neighbors times one minus threshold. Camera-z candidates are bounded by the object box. A second search uses one tenth of the coarse step around the winning sample. Synthetic coarse spacing is 5 mm; real spacing is 2.5 mm. Missing depth remains invalid.')
h('Fusion and differences from the article')
p('The fusion grid uses 4 mm voxels and a 16 mm surface band. Each voxel is projected into the depth maps, using nearest-pixel depth lookup. The measured camera-z minus voxel camera-z is normalized by the band width and accumulated with confidence weights. Marching cubes extracts zero crossings. Faces unsupported by a fully observed voxel cell are rejected. This differs from VripPack: no range-mesh scan conversion, grazing-angle weight, boundary weight or isolated-triangle rejection is reproduced. The coarse grid limits detail and should not be interpreted as matching the paper\'s submillimeter benchmark performance.')
h('3 Quantitative results')
rows=[['Run','MAE mm','P90 mm','Coverage','Seconds','Faces']]
for r in records:
 fmt=lambda k: 'N/A' if r.get(k) is None else f'{r[k]:.3f}'
 rows.append([r['run'],fmt('mae_mm'),fmt('p90_mm'),fmt('foreground_coverage'),f"{r['seconds']:.1f}",str(r['faces'])])
t=Table(rows,colWidths=[92,65,65,65,65,70],repeatRows=1);t.setStyle(TableStyle([('BACKGROUND',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.HexColor('#17365D')),('TEXTCOLOR',(0,0),(-1,0),colors.white),('GRID',(0,0),(-1,-1),.4,colors.lightgrey),('FONTSIZE',(0,0),(-1,-1),9),('BOTTOMPADDING',(0,0),(-1,-1),7)]));story.append(t)
p('MAE and P90 measure absolute camera-z error on jointly valid foreground pixels. Coverage is the fraction of ground-truth foreground pixels with an estimated depth, not surface completeness. Timing includes stereo generation only; fusion, plotting and report generation add time. Background predictions are counted separately in metrics.json. Real-image accuracy is N/A because the original ground truth is not distributed. These metrics cannot be directly compared with the paper\'s mesh-to-surface accuracy or completeness.')
h('4 Qualitative results and discussion')
lookup={r['run']:r for r in records}
if all(k in lookup for k in ['baseline','threshold04','threshold08','textureless']):
 b=lookup['baseline']; low=lookup['threshold04']; high=lookup['threshold08']
 p(f"The baseline foreground depth MAE is {b['mae_mm']:.2f} mm at {100*b['foreground_coverage']:.1f}% coverage. Raising the NCC threshold to 0.8 reduces MAE to {high['mae_mm']:.2f} mm but reduces coverage to {100*high['foreground_coverage']:.1f}%. Lowering it to 0.4 increases coverage to {100*low['foreground_coverage']:.1f}% while increasing MAE to {low['mae_mm']:.2f} mm. This experiment supports the expected accuracy-coverage tradeoff on this scene. It does not establish the same effect on all objects.")
 p(f"The baseline also predicts {b['background_predictions']} pixels outside the true foreground across all views. Boundary-related false matches remain a material failure. The textureless run has {lookup['textureless']['mae_mm']:.2f} mm MAE on the subset it predicts; its output should not be interpreted as successful reconstruction of an untextured object.")
for name in ['baseline','window9','textureless','real']:
 f=R/'results'/name/'diagnostics.png'
 if f.exists():story.append(Image(str(f),width=475,height=119));p(name+': input, estimated depth, confidence and accepted correlation. Invalid depth pixels are blank. Depth scales are automatic; use numerical metrics for quantitative comparison.')
p('Large patches can improve support while blending boundaries; smaller patches can preserve local detail but produce ambiguous correlations. Threshold changes affect both accepted coverage and error. High confidence is not a calibrated probability. The textureless scene may retain silhouette-based false matches even when most constant patches are rejected. Reported error must always be read alongside foreground coverage and background predictions.')
for name in ['baseline','real']:
 f=R/'results'/name/'mesh.png'
 if f.exists():story.append(Image(str(f),width=260,height=260));p(name+' reconstructed mesh. Views and axes are for inspection; no ground-truth alignment or rescaling was fitted.')
p('Limitations: only one synthetic geometry and one reduced-resolution real subset are tested. Sparse viewpoints, repeated procedural texture, fixed square image patches and coarse fusion can create missing or incorrect surfaces. The study does not reproduce all original datasets, reflection experiments or original volumetric weighting. No claim of official benchmark equivalence is made. TA agreement is still needed for this scope and evaluation substitution.')
h('5 Reproducibility checklist')
p('Code and tests are included. Install the pinned requirements in a virtual environment, then run bash run.sh. Seeds are fixed to zero; the procedural renderer is deterministic. run.sh regenerates the experiments and this report. Hardware, Python and platform are recorded in results/environment.json. Expected measurements are in the saved metrics files. Run python -m pytest tests -q to check geometry, NCC, voting, interpolation and fusion. Floating-point and runtime variation across platforms is expected.')
h('References')
p('Goesele et al., CVPR 2006, supplied paper.pdf. Curless and Levoy, A Volumetric Method for Building Complex Models from Range Images, SIGGRAPH 1996. Official dataset and evaluation information: https://vision.middlebury.edu/mview/ and https://vision.middlebury.edu/mview/data/ (checked 3 November 2026). The benchmark states that ground truth is not distributed and submissions are closed.')
SimpleDocTemplate(str(R/'report.pdf'),rightMargin=50,leftMargin=50,topMargin=45,bottomMargin=45).build(story,onFirstPage=page,onLaterPages=page)
